WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.380
Privilege separation should be tested directly.

2
00:00:03.480 --> 00:00:21.480
Tests should verify that low-privilege code cannot perform sensitive operations directly, invalid IPC requests are rejected, malformed messages fail safely, unauthorized callers are denied, path boundaries hold, helpers shut down cleanly, and failures do not leave privileged state behind.

3
00:00:21.760 --> 00:00:24.320
Negative tests are especially important.

4
00:00:24.420 --> 00:00:37.300
A feature may work correctly for an allowed request but still behave poorly when the caller is not authorized, the message version is wrong, the target path is outside policy, the helper is restarting, or a resource is unavailable.

5
00:00:37.400 --> 00:00:43.240
These paths are part of the security contract and should be exercised intentionally.

6
00:00:43.520 --> 00:00:49.120
Monitoring and logs should show privileged request outcomes without leaking sensitive data.

7
00:00:49.220 --> 00:00:56.900
Operators need to know whether requests are allowed, denied, malformed, unsupported, or failing due to dependency problems.

8
00:00:57.000 --> 00:01:04.360
Logs should include safe identifiers and event categories rather than secrets, private handles, or unnecessary system detail.

9
00:01:04.640 --> 00:01:14.720
Code review should focus on authority, caller identity, message validation, resource access, helper lifecycle, update paths, and error behavior.

10
00:01:14.820 --> 00:01:18.900
Privilege changes should be treated as security-relevant changes.

11
00:01:19.000 --> 00:01:27.160
A new helper command, entitlement, service account permission, or deployment rule can alter the boundary even if the code change looks small.

12
00:01:27.440 --> 00:01:30.160
Findings should become tracked engineering work.

13
00:01:30.260 --> 00:01:38.980
When a boundary test fails or monitoring shows unclear behavior, teams should update the contract, implementation, and documentation together.

14
00:01:39.080 --> 00:01:44.040
Privilege separation stays effective only when it is maintained across releases.
